/-!
# Basic orbits and a base of the second Janko group

The degree-100 ATLAS model `TauCeti.Sporadic.J2` has basic-orbit lengths `100, 63, 24, 4` along
the base `0, 1, 2, 3`, and its four-point stabilizer is trivial (`stabilizerChain_four_eq_bot`).
Together these reduce `Nat.card J2` along the stabilizer chain and determine the orders of its
three-point stabilizer (`4`) and two-point stabilizer (`96`).

Explicit words in the two ATLAS generators witness the orbit lower bounds, including an
order-four element generating the three-point stabilizer. For the upper bounds and base rigidity,
a binary relation `orbital` on the 100 points is checked to be invariant under both generators.
Its row at `0` bounds the second orbit (`63`), the points other than `1` in that row and outside
the row at `1` bound the third orbit (`24`), and iterative anchor profiles show that every
`orbital`-preserving permutation fixing `0, 1, 2, 3` is trivial. Common-neighbor counts with
`{0, 1, 2}` restrict the image of `3` under the three-point stabilizer to eight points; the four
candidates outside `{3, 6, 41, 56}` are excluded by composing with an explicit odd involution of
`orbital` fixing `0, 1, 2`, since every element of `J2` is an even permutation
(`le_alternatingGroup`). All finite tables are private proof data checked in the Lean kernel.

## References

* R. A. Wilson, R. A. Parker, J. N. Bray et al., *ATLAS of Finite Group Representations*,
  version 3, representation `J2G1-p100B0`, <https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/v3/>.
* Á. Seress, *Permutation Group Algorithms*, Cambridge Tracts in Mathematics 152,
  Cambridge University Press, 2003, §4.1 (basic orbits and the order formula).
-/

public section

